<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gunung Kawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gunung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> is an 11th-century temple complex at Tampaksiring, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, where ten shrine facades about seven metres high are carved into cliffs on both sides of the Pakerisan river. They are traditionally held to be memorials to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anak-wungsu">Anak Wungsu</a> and members of his family. The site is part of Bali's UNESCO-listed cultural landscape.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunung-kawi.jpg" alt="Gunung Kawi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bali_-_Gunung_Kawi_(2025)_-_img_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
